  "query_preview": "..\n\nActive early September weather pattern continues one more day as the \nnorthwest flow remains over the region, downstream of the strong \nPlains ridge which will reamplify today. Currently, the stalled \nfront remains draped across southern MI but has been muddled a bit \nby the large convective complex that rolled through Thursday \nafternoon and evening. That complex also pushed the main instability \ngradient westward. The next mid level wave is already initiating \nconvection over WI which will work through SE MI early Friday \nmorning. This wave will have skinny elevated CAPE and moderate mid \nlevel lapse rates but alot of moisture with PWATs still running \naround 2 inches and surface dewpoints pushing 70 again. Though the \ndeveloping complex will be outrunning the instability, expectations \nare for the low level jet to help keep activity going into SE MI \nthis morning. And if the WI convection can generate a decent cold \npool to help it along, that would help increase coverage and \nstrength of storms this morning. Combing through all the CAMs, the \nwindow for morning convection looks to be around 10-16Z with a large \namount of variability. SPC Day 1 outlook just came out with pocket \nof Slight Risk south of a line from Grand Haven to Lake St Clair, \ngiving credit to the strong cells currently over WI. We really need \nto see how things line up locally today, how much can the \ninstability gradient push back into SE MI and where does the front \ncome back together. Could very well see the morning convection \nreinforce the gradient to the west and south, preventing the best \nCAPE from advecting back in. Regardless...we're looking for a round \nof convection this morning, possibly lingering toward noon. QPF \nvalues range from 0.5 - 1 inch across the area with the highest \ntotals south of I69. After the morning convection, we remain under \nthe active 850-700mb frontal band with weak low level jet still \ngoing and all the moisture still so expecting scattered showers to \ncontinue through the afternoon. Wind and hail will be possible with \nthe strongest cells, but flooding may become a primary threat as \nwe've now had 2 days of heavy rain and todays showers and storms \nwill again be primed for heavy rain. Any training of storms, \nespecially over those areas previously hit, may result in urban and \nsmall stream flooding. \n\nConfidence goes way down for afternoon/evening storm chances. \nOutflow from earlier convection and surface frontal placement will \ndictate where the next area of convection can initiate. In addition, \nthere are solutions that advertise the elevated front continues to \nactivate. Lack of stronger mid level forcing should help minimize \nchances for larger convective complexes and stronger storms from \ndeveloping. Will have to see how the morning convection plays out \nfirst. \n\nWith the front sagging back southward, may locations will be cooler \ntoday with highs 80 or less. Locations around the Ohio border will \nbe south of the front the longest with less chance of early day \nconvection so temps may again rise into the mid 80s. With the high \ndewpoints lingering one more day, heat indices may touch 90 briefly \nthis afternoon. High pressure builds south across the region this \nweekend into next week which will bring cooler temps the rest of the \nweekend and into next week with highs in the upper 70s.",
